begin model

#@title: Calvin cycle isotopomer labeling, published fluxes (Mu et al., 2010)

#@description: |
#  Dynamic 13C isotopomer labeling of the Calvin cycle, the worked example of
#  Sec. 15.4 in Mu et al. (2010). Thirteen internal reactions, identified by
#  KEGG reaction number, connect fourteen metabolite pools: RuBisCO (R00024)
#  fixes CO2 onto D-ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate to give two molecules of
#  3-phospho-D-glycerate, the reductive branch (R01512, R01063, R01015) carries
#  those to triose phosphates, and the regenerative branch (aldolases R01068
#  and R01829, phosphatases R00762 and R01845, transketolases R01067 and
#  R01641, epimerase R01529, isomerase R01056, and kinase R01523) rebuilds
#  D-ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate through the four-, five-, six-, and
#  seven-carbon sugar phosphates. Six exchange reactions close the system: a
#  CO2 input flux and sinks draining T3P, F6P, R5P, E4P, and G3P.
#
#  Every metabolite carries one component per carbon, in the InChI atom
#  ordering of the fate-map database of Mu et al. (2007), with state 0 for 12C
#  and 1 for 13C. Each rule states the carbon atom fate map explicitly using
#  BNGL component labels (%1, %2, ...), which force the reactant-to-product
#  atom mapping; the transketolases and aldolases split and recombine carbon
#  skeletons, and the epimerase, isomerase, and kinase steps permute them.
#  BioNetGen expands the eighteen rules into the isotopomer balance equations
#  and generates all 562 isotopomers of the network. Rate laws are
#  flux/(pool size) for each reactant pool, so each rule carries its specified
#  flux and pool sizes are preserved; all pool sizes are set to 1.
#
#  This variant differs from the primary model only in carrying the flux values
#  exactly as printed, v_R01529_b = 0.82 and vs_R5P = 0.05. At that setting the
#  X5P balance is 0.01 out of true, the pool vector is no longer a null
#  eigenvector of the network, and the chapter's own 10,000-unit equilibration
#  destroys the metabolic steady state: every metabolite pool decays to zero,
#  falling below 1/e of its nominal size by t = 440 and then decaying
#  exponentially with a time constant of about 370 time units, while CO2, whose
#  only consumer is RuBisCO, grows without bound once R5DP is gone. Nothing is
#  left by the end of the equilibration. The labeling protocol would then divide
#  zero by zero, so it is omitted. The file is kept as the literal published
#  input and as the demonstration of why specified-pool isotopomer models
#  require an exactly balanced flux set; use the primary model for the labeling
#  dynamics.

#@keyword: |
#  metabolic flux analysis, 13C labeling, isotopomer, carbon fate map,
#  atom mapping, Calvin cycle, carbon fixation, photosynthesis, RuBisCO,
#  transketolase, pentose phosphate, isotopically nonstationary labeling,
#  mass action, ordinary differential equations, dimensionless

#@reference: |
#  Mu F, Bauer AL, Faeder JR, Hlavacek WS (2010). Using systems biology
#  techniques to determine metabolic fluxes and metabolite pool sizes. In:
#  Handbook of Chemoinformatics Algorithms, Faulon J-L, Bender A, eds.
#  Chapman and Hall/CRC, Boca Raton, pp. 399-422.
#  ISBN 9781420082920. Sec. 15.4 and Fig. 15.5.
#  Mu F, Williams RF, Unkefer CJ, Unkefer PJ, Faeder JR, Hlavacek WS (2007).
#  Carbon-fate maps for metabolic reactions. Bioinformatics 23:3193-3199.
#  doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btm498

#@note: |
#  Units are arbitrary but consistent: pool sizes are amounts (a.u.), fluxes
#  are amounts per unit time, and a flux divided by the pool sizes of its
#  reactants is a rate constant. Species amounts are continuous isotopomer
#  fractions scaled by pool size, so molecule counts are not meaningful and the
#  model is ODE-only.

#@note: |
#  Metabolite abbreviations follow Fig. 15.5 in Mu et al. (2010): R5P is
#  D-ribulose 5-phosphate, R5DP is D-ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate, Ri5P is
#  D-ribose 5-phosphate, X5P is D-xylulose 5-phosphate, G3P is
#  3-phospho-D-glycerate, P13G is 3-phospho-D-glyceroyl phosphate, T3P is
#  D-glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate, DHAP is glycerone phosphate, F16P and F6P are
#  D-fructose 1,6-bisphosphate and 6-phosphate, E4P is D-erythrose
#  4-phosphate, and S17P and S7P are D-sedoheptulose 1,7-bisphosphate and
#  7-phosphate. The abbreviation list in the caption of Fig. 15.5 glosses R5P
#  twice; the phosphoribulokinase step R01523, R5P -> R5DP, fixes R5P as
#  D-ribulose 5-phosphate.

#@note: |
#  Transcribed from the BioNetGen input file printed in Sec. 15.4 of Mu et al.
#  (2010) with two adaptations:
#  - The CO2 feed is written there as a pair of rules I -> I + CO2 driven by an
#    inert species I held at 1. BioNetGen's null species is used here instead,
#    so the generated network contains exactly the 562 isotopomers the chapter
#    reports rather than those plus I.
#  - The printed observables divide a pattern by V_T3P, which BNGL does not
#    allow; the division is done here by the functions T3P_M0 through T3P_M3.

#@note: |
#  The published fluxes are reported to two decimals and, at that precision,
#  thirteen of the fourteen pool balances close but X5P does not: its inflow
#  v_R01067_f + v_R01641_f + v_R01529_f = 1.03 is 0.01 short of its outflow
#  v_R01067_b + v_R01641_b + v_R01529_b = 1.04, and the five sinks drain 1.05
#  carbon per unit time against a CO2 input of 1.00 (the same 0.01 counted over
#  the five carbons of X5P). The primary model closes both balances by lowering
#  v_R01529_b to 0.81 and vs_R5P to 0.04, one unit in the last reported digit
#  of each; this file keeps the printed values.

begin parameters
  # Metabolite pool sizes; Mu et al. (2010) use the default pool size of one
  V_R5P   1.0  # a.u.
  V_R5DP  1.0  # a.u.
  V_CO2   1.0  # a.u.
  V_G3P   1.0  # a.u.
  V_P13G  1.0  # a.u.
  V_T3P   1.0  # a.u.
  V_DHAP  1.0  # a.u.
  V_F16P  1.0  # a.u.
  V_F6P   1.0  # a.u.
  V_E4P   1.0  # a.u.
  V_X5P   1.0  # a.u.
  V_Ri5P  1.0  # a.u.
  V_S17P  1.0  # a.u.
  V_S7P   1.0  # a.u.

  # Internal fluxes, by KEGG reaction number; _f and _b are the forward and
  # backward fluxes of a reaction treated as reversible
  v_R00024    1.00  # a.u./time
  v_R01512    1.95  # a.u./time
  v_R01063_f  0.05  # a.u./time
  v_R01063_b  2.00  # a.u./time
  v_R01015_f  0.86  # a.u./time
  v_R01015_b  0.10  # a.u./time
  v_R01068_f  0.15  # a.u./time
  v_R01068_b  0.58  # a.u./time
  v_R00762    0.43  # a.u./time
  v_R01067_f  0.50  # a.u./time
  v_R01067_b  0.12  # a.u./time
  v_R01829_f  0.20  # a.u./time
  v_R01829_b  0.53  # a.u./time
  v_R01845    0.33  # a.u./time
  v_R01641_f  0.43  # a.u./time
  v_R01641_b  0.10  # a.u./time
  v_R01529_f  0.10  # a.u./time
  v_R01529_b  0.82  # a.u./time
  v_R01056_f  0.50  # a.u./time
  v_R01056_b  0.17  # a.u./time
  v_R01523    1.00  # a.u./time

  # Sink fluxes draining the five pools that feed central metabolism
  vs_T3P  0.05  # a.u./time
  vs_F6P  0.05  # a.u./time
  vs_R5P  0.05  # a.u./time
  vs_E4P  0.05  # a.u./time
  vs_G3P  0.05  # a.u./time

  # CO2 input flux, split into its unlabeled and 13C-labeled parts. The
  # labeling protocol switches these to 0.9 and 0.1.
  v_vi_CO2_unlabeled  1  # a.u./time
  v_vi_CO2_labeled    0  # a.u./time
end parameters

begin molecule types
  # One component per carbon, indexed in InChI order; state 0 is 12C, 1 is 13C
  CO2(C1~0~1)
  # Three-carbon intermediates of the reductive branch
  G3P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1)
  P13G(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1)
  T3P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1)
  DHAP(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1)
  # Four-carbon sugar phosphate of the regenerative branch
  E4P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1)
  # Five-carbon sugar phosphates; R5DP is the RuBisCO substrate
  R5P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1,C5~0~1)
  R5DP(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1,C5~0~1)
  Ri5P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1,C5~0~1)
  X5P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1,C5~0~1)
  # Six-carbon sugar phosphates
  F16P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1,C5~0~1,C6~0~1)
  F6P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1,C5~0~1,C6~0~1)
  # Seven-carbon sugar phosphates
  S17P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1,C5~0~1,C6~0~1,C7~0~1)
  S7P(C1~0~1,C2~0~1,C3~0~1,C4~0~1,C5~0~1,C6~0~1,C7~0~1)
end molecule types

begin seed species
  # Every pool starts fully unlabeled at its specified pool size
  CO2(C1~0)  V_CO2  # a.u.
  G3P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0)   V_G3P   # a.u.
  P13G(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0)  V_P13G  # a.u.
  T3P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0)   V_T3P   # a.u.
  DHAP(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0)  V_DHAP  # a.u.
  E4P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0)  V_E4P  # a.u.
  R5P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0,C5~0)   V_R5P   # a.u.
  R5DP(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0,C5~0)  V_R5DP  # a.u.
  Ri5P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0,C5~0)  V_Ri5P  # a.u.
  X5P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0,C5~0)   V_X5P   # a.u.
  F16P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0,C5~0,C6~0)  V_F16P  # a.u.
  F6P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0,C5~0,C6~0)   V_F6P   # a.u.
  S17P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0,C5~0,C6~0,C7~0)  V_S17P  # a.u.
  S7P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0,C4~0,C5~0,C6~0,C7~0)   V_S7P   # a.u.
end seed species

begin observables
  # T3P mass isotopomers, the readout chosen in Sec. 15.4 of Mu et al. (2010);
  # M0 through M3 group the eight T3P isotopomers by number of 13C atoms
  Molecules Obs_T3P_M0 T3P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~0)
  Molecules Obs_T3P_M1 T3P(C1~1,C2~0,C3~0),T3P(C1~0,C2~1,C3~0),\
    T3P(C1~0,C2~0,C3~1)
  Molecules Obs_T3P_M2 T3P(C1~1,C2~1,C3~0),T3P(C1~1,C2~0,C3~1),\
    T3P(C1~0,C2~1,C3~1)
  Molecules Obs_T3P_M3 T3P(C1~1,C2~1,C3~1)

  # 13C atoms in the T3P and F6P pools; one match per labeled carbon
  Molecules Obs_C13_T3P T3P(C1~1),T3P(C2~1),T3P(C3~1)
  Molecules Obs_C13_F6P F6P(C1~1),F6P(C2~1),F6P(C3~1),F6P(C4~1),F6P(C5~1),\
    F6P(C6~1)

  # Pool sizes. With the printed fluxes these do not hold at 1: every
  # metabolite pool decays toward zero and CO2 accumulates without bound.
  Molecules Obs_Tot_CO2  CO2()
  Molecules Obs_Tot_T3P  T3P()
  Molecules Obs_Tot_F6P  F6P()
  Molecules Obs_Tot_X5P  X5P()
  Molecules Obs_Tot_R5DP R5DP()
end observables

begin functions
  # T3P mass isotopomer fractions, the observables of Sec. 15.4 in Mu et al.
  # (2010). At isotopic steady state these approach the binomial distribution
  # for three carbons at the feed enrichment.
  T3P_M0() Obs_T3P_M0/V_T3P
  T3P_M1() Obs_T3P_M1/V_T3P
  T3P_M2() Obs_T3P_M2/V_T3P
  T3P_M3() Obs_T3P_M3/V_T3P

  # Fractional 13C labeling per carbon. CO2 is the only carbon input, so both
  # approach the enrichment of the CO2 feed.
  FL_T3P() Obs_C13_T3P/(3*Obs_Tot_T3P)
  FL_F6P() Obs_C13_F6P/(6*Obs_Tot_F6P)
end functions

begin reaction rules
  # CO2 input flux, split into unlabeled and 13C-labeled feed
  R_source_CO2_unlabeled: 0 -> CO2(C1~0) v_vi_CO2_unlabeled
  R_source_CO2_labeled:   0 -> CO2(C1~1) v_vi_CO2_labeled

  # R00024, ribulose-bisphosphate carboxylase: the carbon fixation step.
  # The CO2 carbon (%6) and three carbons of R5DP form one G3P; the other
  # two R5DP carbons form the second G3P.
  R00024: R5DP(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5) + CO2(C1%6) -> \
    G3P(C1%2,C2%4,C3%6) + G3P(C1%1,C2%3,C3%5) v_R00024/(V_R5DP*V_CO2)

  # R01512, phosphoglycerate kinase; carbon order preserved
  R01512: G3P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3) -> P13G(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3) v_R01512/V_G3P

  # R01063, glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase; carbons cycled
  R01063: T3P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3) <-> P13G(C1%2,C2%3,C3%1) \
    v_R01063_f/V_T3P, v_R01063_b/V_P13G

  # R01015, triose-phosphate isomerase; carbon order preserved
  R01015: T3P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3) <-> DHAP(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3) \
    v_R01015_f/V_T3P, v_R01015_b/V_DHAP

  # R01068, fructose-bisphosphate aldolase; splits a hexose into two trioses
  R01068: F16P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5,C6%6) <-> \
    DHAP(C1%5,C2%2,C3%6) + T3P(C1%4,C2%1,C3%3) \
    v_R01068_f/V_F16P, v_R01068_b/(V_DHAP*V_T3P)

  # R00762, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase; carbon order preserved
  R00762: F16P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5,C6%6) -> \
    F6P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5,C6%6) v_R00762/V_F16P

  # R01067, transketolase; moves a two-carbon unit from F6P onto T3P
  R01067: F6P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5,C6%6) + T3P(C1%7,C2%8,C3%9) <-> \
    E4P(C1%5,C2%1,C3%4,C4%3) + X5P(C1%2,C2%8,C3%6,C4%9,C5%7) \
    v_R01067_f/(V_F6P*V_T3P), v_R01067_b/(V_E4P*V_X5P)

  # R01829, sedoheptulose-bisphosphate aldolase; splits a heptose
  R01829: S17P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5,C6%6,C7%7) <-> \
    DHAP(C1%6,C2%2,C3%4) + E4P(C1%7,C2%1,C3%5,C4%3) \
    v_R01829_f/V_S17P, v_R01829_b/(V_DHAP*V_E4P)

  # R01845, sedoheptulose-bisphosphatase; carbons permuted pairwise
  R01845: S17P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5,C6%6,C7%7) -> \
    S7P(C1%2,C2%1,C3%4,C4%3,C5%6,C6%5,C7%7) v_R01845/V_S17P

  # R01641, transketolase; moves a two-carbon unit from S7P onto T3P
  R01641: S7P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5,C6%6,C7%7) + T3P(C1%8,C2%9,C3%10) <-> \
    Ri5P(C1%2,C2%4,C3%6,C4%7,C5%5) + X5P(C1%1,C2%9,C3%3,C4%10,C5%8) \
    v_R01641_f/(V_S7P*V_T3P), v_R01641_b/(V_Ri5P*V_X5P)

  # R01529, ribulose-phosphate 3-epimerase; carbon order preserved
  R01529: R5P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5) <-> X5P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5) \
    v_R01529_f/V_R5P, v_R01529_b/V_X5P

  # R01056, ribose-5-phosphate isomerase; carbons permuted
  R01056: Ri5P(C1%1,C2%2,C3%3,C4%4,C5%5) <-> \
    R5P(C1%5,C2%1,C3%4,C4%2,C5%3) v_R01056_f/V_Ri5P, v_R01056_b/V_R5P

  # R01523, phosphoribulokinase; regenerates the RuBisCO substrate
  R01523: R5P(C1%11,C2%12,C3%13,C4%14,C5%15) -> \
    R5DP(C1%12,C2%11,C3%14,C4%13,C5%15) v_R01523/V_R5P

  # Sinks feeding central metabolism; see Fig. 15.5 in Mu et al. (2010)
  R_sink_T3P: T3P() -> 0 vs_T3P/V_T3P
  R_sink_F6P: F6P() -> 0 vs_F6P/V_F6P
  R_sink_R5P: R5P() -> 0 vs_R5P/V_R5P
  R_sink_E4P: E4P() -> 0 vs_E4P/V_E4P
  R_sink_G3P: G3P() -> 0 vs_G3P/V_G3P
end reaction rules

end model

begin actions
  generate_network({overwrite=>1})  # 562 species, 4042 reactions

  # Washout of the metabolic steady state under the printed fluxes

  #@protocol: |
  #  Step 4 of the recipe in Sec. 15.4 of Mu et al. (2010), the 10,000-unit
  #  equilibration on an unlabeled CO2 feed, sampled finely enough to show what
  #  it does with the printed flux values. Every metabolite Obs_Tot_ observable
  #  starts at 1, is below 1/e by t = 440, and decays exponentially thereafter
  #  with a time constant of about 370 time units, while Obs_Tot_CO2 grows
  #  linearly once RuBisCO has run out of R5DP. Compare with the primary model,
  #  where all pools hold at 1 for the whole 10,000 units.

  simulate({method=>"ode",suffix=>"ode_equil",t_start=>0,t_end=>10000,\
    n_steps=>1000,print_functions=>1})
end actions
